non-minimalists  alicia writes in i've changed to a job  that makes me a lot happier but a lot  less pay  my family has had to make changes  because of it what are some changes one  can make  to make a change like this more  sustainable it's a lot of change  put that change in a jar well  here here's i'll give you the pithy  answer and then we'll talk about it a  little bit because  this is the biggest change i had to make  in my own life and i talked about it in  everything that remains  um so my pithy answer is debt free  is the new pay raise amen and that's the  one thing to think about like  if you have all of these debts if you  have student loan debt if you have car  debt you know you got this car payment  every month  you're paying for this oversized house  maybe it's a mortgage maybe it's just a  luxury condo or whatever  you're paying a big payment over there  maybe you're paying for the credit card  debt that you have  or you're paying for you know the the  the uh  all these other um bills that we have  right  debt payments yes they are just debt  payments but debt free is the new pay  raise because if you get rid of that  debt you've automatically given yourself  a pay raise so uh if you don't owe  anybody  anything then you give yourself the  freedom  to do anything yeah you can tweet that  too sean  it's funny because like i i don't get it  as often  but i used to get it a lot when we first  started the minimalist people would be  like oh well it's easy for you  because you know you walked away from  the corporate world and you obviously  had you know  uh thousands and thousands of dollars  saved up  and uh you know you're single and you  know it's all these like  these assumptions excuses yeah excuses  and ultimately i would just say to those  people like no no  it's easy for me because i spend less  money than what i make and it's not it's  not about having  thousands and thousands of dollars saved  up certainly it's it's it's it is a  great idea to have a safety net and to  save for retirement i'm not trying to  say that those things are important  but what i'm saying is is ultimately if  you want to  uh have the freedom if you want to be  financially secure you have got to start  spending less money than what you make  today  it's okay to sacrifice your comfort  as long as you're not sacrificing your  values yes and i think that's one of the  things we're spending money on all these  like creature comforts  and and we're actually stepping all over  our values and doing things that don't  align with the person we want to become  and by the way bravo to alicia here  for stepping in that direction and yeah  becoming the person she wants to be  even if it means making a little bit  less money or a lot less money in the  process yeah congratulations alicia  uh my my short answer is this if we put  money  first a meaningful life becomes the  opportunity cost  so what i mean by that for for alicia is  sometimes  we might take this pay decrease so we  can live a more meaningful life  and then once we take that pay decrease  we start to feel  the the pains or the discomfort not pain  but maybe discomfort of those  those uh those comforts that maybe we  have to give up  yeah but the thing is is to  to really feel good about the decision  that alicia's made  she she has to remind herself  how she gave up that  you know piece of her salary she gave up  the comforts yeah so she could live a  more meaningful life  a comfortable life is not a meaningful  life and here's how i look at it man  like when i  so i i don't know i made 150 or 175 000  bucks that year that last  year i left um the corporate world right  did not have thousands of dollars saved  up uh  had nothing to show for the salary that  i was making for all those years  but what i'll say though is that when  the next year when i made like 27 000  bucks 28 000 bucks i forget how much it  was  i i legitimately kind of had this  conversation with myself like man like  i made so much less money this year  and i've never been happier and like i  have never i  i traveled more that year than i had  ever traveled uh thanks to  readers letting us stay on their floor  and uh you know  us sleeping in our car i mean it wasn't  like we spent you know tens of thousands  of dollars to to go on that book tour  but but i i would sit there and i i sat  there and i remember asking myself like  okay  um i made 100 and called 150 000  less or 125 000 less than the previous  year  would i pay someone a hundred and if i  had the money  if i if i could afford it would i pay  someone 125  000 to relive that year  and i absolutely would have and that's  that's what it comes down to so  alicia if you ever start to feel the  pain you've got to ask yourself like and  when i say the pain the pain of getting  rid of those comforts you got to ask  yourself and say okay if i'm  if i'm making ten thousand dollars less  a year you got to ask yourself what i  spend or would i pay someone 10 000  if it would alleviate the stress of  making that extra ten thousand dollars  if it would allow me to live a more  meaningful life with my family  the answer is usually going to be yes  yeah if the answer is no then yeah do  something  do something different um you know  minimalism isn't about deprivation it's  not about uh just living with as little  as possible it's about living  intentionally yeah and asking yourselves  questions like that are going to help  you live more intentional  alicia i'd love to send you a copy of  our book essential it's an essay  collection  with 150 essays 12 different chapters  about  12 areas of intentional living and  there's an entire chapter in here about  finances including the plan that you and  i went through to  make sure we got out of debt and more  importantly stayed all the debt once we  got there  that's that's the like the important  next step but also investing in in your  future as well  even on a a smaller or much  smaller income so i hope you enjoyed  that copy of the central sean if you  could reach out to her give her the  audiobook  the book book or the ebook whichever  she'd like

